  "id": "hijazi_majass_sahba",
  "name": "Majass and sahba (Hijazi urban song)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Urban vocal art of Mecca, Medina, Jeddah and Taif built on the Arabic maqam, centred on the majass, an unmetred sung improvisation on lines of poetry, together with the dana and the sahba, a group song sung at weddings and gatherings. Singers such as Mohammed Ali Sindi carried the repertoire into the record and radio era, and later Hijazi singers drew on it; the fiddle card stands for the violin of its oud-led ensembles.",
  "recipe": "classic early-1940s majass and sahba, art Music, middle eastern classical branch. neutral third half flat, Arabic maqam, solo with instrumental accompaniment, arab mawwal layali maqam modulation. oud, fiddle, credited frame drums. am radio booth. Neumann U47, German V72 tube pre, German tube console, Telefunken u73b broadcast comp. tape 15ips hall reverb.",
